snippet.description:
Heavy snow shut down Interstate 80 over Donner Summit, California, in both directions on Friday, November 13, 2026, as the first major storm of the season hit Lake Tahoe and the Sierra Nevada. Spinouts closed the pass shortly after 4 PM, drivers were held at Nyack, Kingvale and Truckee, chain controls went up from Nyack to the Nevada state line, and eastbound drivers were still being stopped at Colfax at 10 PM. Forecasters expected snow at 2 to 3 inches an hour, with bursts of 4 inches an hour up high.

Truckee, Tahoe City and South Lake Tahoe woke up under fresh snow. Carson Pass had a foot on the ground, Ebbetts Pass picked up 10 new inches and Heavenly measured 7 inches. Tioga Road and Glacier Point Road in Yosemite, Sonora Pass and Highway 180 into Kings Canyon were closed, and heavy snow fell in Mammoth Lakes.

A second storm brought more snow to Lake Tahoe on Sunday night and Monday with ridge gusts near 70 mph, and the National Weather Service has issued winter storm watches from Mount Shasta to Sequoia National Park for a third storm late Tuesday through Thursday, with around 4 feet possible above 8,000 feet and travel described as very difficult to impossible.

In this video, we examine the Interstate 80 closure over Donner Pass, how Caltrans chain controls work, the snow totals in Truckee, South Lake Tahoe, Carson Pass, Ebbetts Pass and Mammoth Lakes, the High Sierra road closures, and what the next two storms could bring.

We also explain why the Sierra Nevada gets so much snow so fast: wet Pacific air is forced up the mountains, cools and drops its moisture, while a falling snow level brings the snow down into the towns.

Daily Weather Report covers natural disasters and extreme weather events across the United States as they develop, using sourced reporting and avoiding unsupported speculation.

